// Small, focused domain specialisation. Each table maps a `Domain` value
// from src/schema.js to a short list of bullets; the helpers below render
// the bullets as a `###` subsection or — for any unspecialised domain —
// return an empty string so the host template stays byte-stable.
//
// To add a new specialised domain:
//   1. Add a key to DOMAIN_RISKS and/or DOMAIN_POSITIONING using a value
//      that exists in DOMAIN_VALUES (the load-time check below will catch
//      typos at server start and during `npm test`).
//   2. Run `npm test` and `npm run generate:examples`. Drift in
//      examples/<id> is only acceptable if that example's domain matches
//      the key you just added.

import { DOMAIN_VALUES } from "../schema.js";

const DOMAIN_RISKS = {
  "climate & sustainability": [
    "Greenwashing exposure: every public claim must be specific and falsifiable. Vague \"sustainable\" copy is a liability, not a marketing asset.",
    "Impact measurability: emissions / impact numbers must declare their methodology, scope (1 / 2 / 3 if applicable), time window, and uncertainty band — never round to a clean number that hides the math.",
    "Data quality: third-party sustainability data is patchy and slow-moving. Document every source and refresh cadence; treat stale data as a bug, not a footnote.",
    "Reporting / regulatory drift: rules (CSRD, SEC climate disclosure, voluntary frameworks) change yearly. Pin the version you support and a re-evaluation date.",
    "Trust and traceability: users will only adopt this if they can show an auditor where each number came from. Audit trail is a feature, not an extra."
  ],
  "professional services": [
    "Trust and credibility: a single embarrassing output destroys word-of-mouth. Slow rollout and a supervised mode beat a fast generic launch.",
    "Client-data privacy: assume PII and privileged information will end up in the system. No third-party logging, conservative retention, clear export + delete on request.",
    "Liability and expectation management: the product assists, it does not give legal / financial / tax / strategic advice. Make this boundary explicit in the UI, not buried in the ToS.",
    "Boundary between software help and professional judgement: the user is still the licensed expert. Avoid UX patterns that nudge them to rubber-stamp model output.",
    "Onboarding for non-technical users: partners / consultants / advisors are domain experts, not power users. First-run flow has to make the daily core action obvious in under three minutes."
  ],
  "health & wellness": [
    "Health-data handling: assume HIPAA-style obligations even outside the US (GDPR Art. 9 covers health data as special-category). Encryption at rest and in transit, minimal retention, audit logs, and a clear data-residency story are entry-cost, not differentiators.",
    "Crisis-path safety: anything that touches mental health, self-harm, or acute medical situations needs a designed and tested escalation path — visible, fast, with real-human routing — *before* launch, not after the first incident.",
    "Off-label use is inevitable: users will reach for the product for diagnosis, dosing, or therapy regardless of what the ToS says. Surface a clear in-product \"this is not medical advice\" line and a documented referral path; ToS-only disclaimers do not survive scrutiny.",
    "Clinical claims = different product: \"treats\", \"diagnoses\", \"cures\" language moves you into MedTech regulation (FDA SaMD, EU MDR). Wellness phrasing is fine; clinical phrasing is a regulated product class with a different launch path.",
    "Trust under bad-news scenarios: a wellness product is judged on the day a user's data leaks or a recommendation contributes to harm. Incident response, user-initiated export, and account deletion must work end-to-end before traffic scales."
  ],
  "finance": [
    "Regulatory drift: applicable regimes (GDPR, MiFID II, PSD2, DORA in the EU; SOX, GLBA, BSA / FinCEN in the US; APRA, FCA, MAS, equivalents elsewhere) update yearly. Pin the version you support, store the policy artefact next to the code, and re-evaluate on a calendar — not when a regulator pings you.",
    "KYC / AML obligations: any product that touches funds, identity verification, or account onboarding inherits Customer Due Diligence and Suspicious-Activity-Report workflows. Do not add \"deposits\" / \"transfers\" / \"wallet\" language until the program is real, documented, and reviewed.",
    "Model risk on any predictive component: if the product scores, recommends, or auto-decides, document training-data lineage, validation methodology, ongoing monitoring, and a deterministic fallback for when the model is unavailable or wrong. Regulators will ask; users will not trust unsigned numbers either.",
    "Audit trail is non-negotiable: every state change captured with who / when / what / why, immutably and exportable. Auditors and customers ask for the same evidence — ship the export path before you ship features that depend on it.",
    "Conservative defaults beat impressive automation: \"we suggested + a human approved\" always wins over \"we did\". The blast radius of a wrong automated decision in finance is measured in dollars and lawsuits — design for reversibility before speed."
  ],
  "food & hospitality": [
    "Seasonality and margin pressure: revenue swings hard between peak (weekend lunch, holidays, summer terraces) and trough. The daily core action has to work brilliantly during 2× rushes or it gets dropped during them. Margin per cover is tight — every minute of operator attention has a measurable cost.",
    "Shift / front-of-house turnover: staff churn in F&B is structurally high (60–100% annual is normal). Product onboarding has to survive a new server's first Friday-night shift, with no read-the-manual moment. Train-by-doing beats train-by-handout.",
    "Allergen and food-safety compliance: allergen labelling, HACCP records, supply-chain traceability (EU 1169/2011, FDA / FSA equivalents), and date-coding are regulatory bedrock — not \"best effort\". A wrong allergen label is a hospitalised customer and a lawsuit, not a UX bug.",
    "Peak-hour reliability is the whole game: two minutes of downtime during a Saturday rush is worse than two hours on Tuesday afternoon. Offline graceful degradation, conservative caching, and a predictable performance budget matter more than feature breadth.",
    "Owner-operator economics: most independents run at single-digit net margins. They will not pay for \"nice to have\" — the product has to demonstrably save labour hours, prevent waste, or unlock revenue per shift. Quantify the saving in the pitch; the operator already does the math."
  ],
  "education": [
    "Student-data privacy is special-category from day one: FERPA (US), GDPR Art. 9 + age-appropriate-design codes (EU / UK), PIPEDA / FOIPPA (Canada), and COPPA's verifiable-parental-consent rule for under-13 in the US. The parent-vs-student split is the hardest part — under-13 is parent-controlled, 13–18 is jurisdiction-dependent, 18+ is student-controlled. Build the consent and data-subject-rights flow before the first feature, not after the first procurement question.",
    "Minor-safety is a duty-of-care surface, not a feature flag: any direct-message, peer-to-peer, or teacher-student channel inherits real safeguarding obligations — moderation, reporting paths, age-gating, and explicit logging of cross-role interactions. \"Family-friendly\" copy is the regulatory floor; the design has to back it up under a school-board audit.",
    "Accessibility for diverse learners is the procurement gate: WCAG 2.2 AA is the entry cost, not a stretch goal. The product must work for screen-reader users, keyboard-only users, students with dyslexia or low vision, and cognitive-load-sensitive learners. Education products that fail an accessibility audit get banned from districts and lose entire revenue lines overnight — this is not a \"v2 polish\" item.",
    "Proctoring and academic-integrity features carry real harm risk: camera-on monitoring, tab-blocking, keystroke patterns, and AI-driven \"cheating detection\" are increasingly regulated and litigated. A wrong flag has lasting consequences on a student's record. Default to assistive, not surveillance; if proctoring ships, the human-review path and the appeal process are part of the product, not a settings toggle.",
    "Outcomes claims are advertising claims: \"raises grades by X%\", \"boosts reading level by Y\" trigger FTC, ED Department, or ASA scrutiny once the product scales. Cite the study, the cohort, the time window, and the comparator. Vague efficacy copy fails school-district procurement and collapses parent trust on the first hard question."
  ],
  "logistics & supply chain": [
    "Driver UX is constrained by law and physics: interacting with a screen while a vehicle is in motion is illegal in most jurisdictions (UK Highway Code, German StVO §23, US state-by-state distracted-driving laws plus FMCSA mobile-phone rules for CDL drivers). Driver-facing surfaces need voice-first or motion-locked interaction modes; UI that requires taps on the move is a regulatory liability and a safety incident waiting to happen.",
    "Hardware-integration failure modes are not just software bugs: ELDs, GPS units, temperature sensors, barcode scanners, dash cams, refrigeration controllers all fail in the real world (battery dead, GPS denied, cellular dropout, sensor drift, BT pairing lost). Treat hardware as untrusted — every reading carries a freshness / confidence stamp, every offline gap has a documented recovery path, every sensor failure has a designed degradation state, not a generic error toast.",
    "Telematics privacy is a real surface: driver location is personal data under GDPR (and increasingly under US state privacy laws — CCPA, CPRA, CO, CT, VA equivalents). The ELD mandate (FMCSA 49 CFR 395) produces an audit trail that is both regulatory ammunition and a privacy surface. Document who can see what, retention windows, off-duty boundaries, and the lawful basis for processing — drivers and unions have pushed back on GPS surveillance more than once, sometimes in court.",
    "Hours-of-Service / DOT / ELD regulatory bedrock: HOS rules (FMCSA 49 CFR 395 in the US, EU 561/2006 + EC 165/2014 tachograph rules in Europe, equivalents in CA / AU / NZ) govern when a driver may legally drive. Any product that schedules, dispatches, or routes drivers must encode these rules, not just display them — auto-dispatching a driver into an HOS violation is a fineable offence for the carrier and a license risk for the driver.",
    "Peak-season reliability is the operational test: Q4 retail (Black Friday → Christmas), back-to-school, harvest, summer tourism — network capacity can 2–3× overnight, queues back up, hardware fails under heat / cold / dust, on-call rotations break. Capacity-test the product against 3× peak load before peak — discovering it during peak is a customer-loss event in a margin-tight industry that does not give second chances."
  ],
  "retail & e-commerce": [
    "Payment-card compliance is non-negotiable: every surface that touches a card number falls under PCI DSS scope (current standard PCI DSS v4.0). 3-D Secure + Strong Customer Authentication (PSD2 / SCA in Europe is mandatory for almost all online card transactions; equivalent regimes elsewhere) are the minimum bar — bypassing them shifts chargeback liability to the merchant. Tokenise through a PCI-DSS-compliant processor (Stripe, Adyen, Worldpay, Braintree); storing PAN data inside the application is an immediate scope expansion that almost no team should take on.",
    "Peak-season and flash-sale reliability is the operational test: Black Friday, Cyber Monday, Singles' Day, Boxing Day push traffic 5–20× over baseline in a multi-hour window, and a flash sale or influencer feature can produce that load with no notice. Inventory race conditions, cart-abandonment cascades, payment-processor rate-limits, and CDN cache stampedes all surface under that load. Capacity-test against 10× peak, document the rate-limit and queuing strategy, and wire up a public status page before the first sale event — not after.",
    "Returns and chargebacks are an adversarial surface: return fraud (worn-and-returned, swap-and-return, \"wardrobing\") and chargeback fraud (\"friendly fraud\" — a legitimate purchase disputed by the cardholder) cost online retailers single-digit percent of revenue, and an incomplete returns workflow becomes a fraud vector overnight. Capture the evidence the dispute system needs (delivery proof, IP / device fingerprint, photos at receipt, return-condition photos) at the moment it's cheap to collect, not when a chargeback arrives 90 days later.",
    "Marketplace-vs-merchant is a regulatory split: running a multi-seller marketplace (independent sellers transacting with end customers) creates obligations distinct from a single-merchant store — VAT / sales-tax collection rules (EU OSS / IOSS, US Wayfair-era state thresholds), DAC7 reporting in the EU, marketplace-facilitator laws in 40+ US states, and product-safety / counterfeit obligations under the EU Digital Services Act and the US INFORM Consumers Act. Decide which side of that line you sit on at design time, not under the first regulator letter.",
    "Storefront accessibility is law, not aspiration: the European Accessibility Act (EAA) is in full effect for B2C e-commerce storefronts since June 2025; ADA Title III litigation against US online retailers has been a steady stream for years (Domino's, Winn-Dixie, etc.). WCAG 2.2 AA on every customer-facing page is the entry cost — product detail, add-to-cart, checkout, payment, and account flows must work for screen-reader users, keyboard-only users, low-vision users, and motor-impaired users. An inaccessible checkout is a chargeback magnet and a lawsuit magnet at the same time."
  ],
  "creative & media": [
    "Rights, licensing, and royalty traceability is a load-bearing surface: every asset has a chain of authorship, licensing terms (Creative Commons variants, work-for-hire, exclusive vs. non-exclusive, geographic + duration restrictions), every derivative, and every payment-split row that must survive a dispute years later. Track those at the database level, not in spreadsheets. For music, the rights-organisation standards (DDEX, CWR) used by ASCAP / BMI / SACEM / GEMA / PRS are the interchange format — emit those from day one, not as a v2 export.",
    "AI-generated content disclosure is now a regulated surface: the EU AI Act (GPAI obligations from August 2025, most provisions from August 2026) requires labelling of AI-generated or AI-manipulated content; California AB 2655 / SB 942 plus 9+ other US state laws codify \"made with AI\" disclosure for political and commercial content; the C2PA / Content Credentials standard (Adobe, Microsoft, BBC, NYT, OpenAI) is the de-facto provenance manifest. If the product touches generated images, audio, or video, support C2PA manifests and visible \"AI-generated\" labels — retrofitting after launch is more expensive than designing them in.",
    "Takedown and notice-and-action response is a contractual obligation: DMCA (US) gives platforms a safe harbour conditional on a 24–48 hour takedown response; the EU Digital Services Act (enforceable for all platforms since 17 February 2024) requires a notice-and-action mechanism, a transparency report, and an appeal route. Build the takedown intake form, the counter-notice path, the human-review queue, and the audit log up front — operating without them is a regulatory and reputational liability waiting on the first viral dispute.",
    "Contributor-vs-platform trust is fragile: every cycle of \"a major platform changed payouts overnight\" (Spotify minimum-stream thresholds, YouTube monetisation tweaks, Substack policy shifts) costs the platform creator goodwill and migration risk. Document the payout formula, give 60+ days' notice on changes that lower a creator's earnings, and never let an algorithmic change ship without a creator-facing changelog. Creators talk; the network effects you spent years building leak fast in this direction.",
    "Copyright + moral-rights jurisdictional patchwork: copyright term, fair-use vs. fair-dealing, moral rights (which exist in the EU / UK / many other jurisdictions, with different transferability rules), and the public-domain calculation differ enough between jurisdictions that a one-rule-for-all engine will produce wrong answers somewhere. Default to per-jurisdiction handling for usage-rights computations, and make the jurisdiction-of-record visible to the user — never silently apply US fair use to a European creator's work."
  ],
  "real estate": [
    "Fair-housing rules are a regulated surface, including on algorithmic decisions: the US Fair Housing Act (FHA) prohibits discrimination on race, colour, religion, national origin, sex, familial status, and disability across listings, advertising, screening, and steering. HUD's 2023 guidance on tenant-screening algorithms made clear that automated decisions still inherit FHA liability — Meta's 2022 settlement on housing ad targeting and SafeRent's 2024 settlement on AI tenant screening set the enforcement precedent. EU equivalents (Race Equality Directive 2000/43/EC, Gender Equality Directive 2004/113/EC, plus national rules in DE / FR / UK / NL) apply similarly. Audit any ranking, matching, or screening logic for protected-class disparate impact at design time, document the mitigation, and never let \"the algorithm decided\" be the explanation of record.",
    "MLS / IDX / portal integration patchwork is a load-bearing dependency: in the US, listing data flows through hundreds of regional MLSs under RESO Web API + RESO Data Dictionary standards, with IDX feed agreements that carry carrier-specific rules on display, retention, and attribution; in the UK + EU, listings flow through Rightmove / Zoopla / OnTheMarket / ImmobilienScout24 / SeLoger with their own contractual rules. Any breach of an IDX agreement (showing a listing past expiry, mis-attributing the listing agent, scraping outside the licensed feed) is a contract termination, not a polite warning.",
    "Listing accuracy and advertising rules are litigation surfaces: square footage / square metre disputes, undisclosed-defect claims, \"stigmatised property\" disclosure rules (state-by-state in the US, member-state-by-member-state in the EU), and outdated photos vs. current condition all generate complaints to state real-estate commissions and consumer-protection regulators. The UK Consumer Protection from Unfair Trading Regulations (CPRs) and the Business Protection from Misleading Marketing Regulations (BPRs), plus Property Misdescriptions Act precedent, treat inaccuracies as actionable misrepresentation. Default to a versioned listing record with a verifiable last-updated timestamp — treat edits as new versions, not in-place mutations.",
    "Dual-agency, agency-licensing, and disclosure rules vary sharply by jurisdiction: dual agency (one agent representing buyer and seller) is illegal in several US states (FL, CO, KS, OK, TX, VT, WY, AK), regulated in others, and requires informed-consent disclosure where allowed; agent-licensing rules (sales-person vs. broker, supervising-broker requirements) are state-by-state in the US and similarly variable across the EU. Anything that connects users with an \"agent\" needs to know which jurisdiction they are in and surface the correct disclosure flow before the introduction, not after.",
    "Anti-money-laundering on high-value transactions is an enforcement priority: FinCEN's beneficial-ownership rule for residential real estate (effective December 2025 in covered metro areas, expanding) requires reporting of all-cash purchases above set thresholds; the EU's AMLD5 / AMLD6 plus the new AML Authority (AMLA, operational since 2025) treat property professionals as obliged entities for KYC, source-of-funds checks, and Suspicious-Activity-Report filing. Any product touching transaction flow, escrow, earnest money, or commission distribution inherits some of that perimeter — design the audit trail, ID-verification path, and lawful-basis records up front, not when the first regulator letter arrives."
  ],
  "non-profit & community": [
    "Charity registration and tax-deductible-donation status vary sharply by jurisdiction: US 501(c)(3) determination + IRS Form 990 annual filing; UK Charity Commission registration + annual return; Germany Gemeinnützigkeit per §52 AO + Freistellungsbescheid; cantonal recognition in Switzerland. Donation receipts must comply with the local regime — US-side acknowledgement-letter requirements differ entirely from German Zuwendungsbestätigung Mustertexte. Building a \"donate\" or \"tax-receipt\" feature without checking the org's legal status in their jurisdiction is a fast path to a compliance violation that can cost the org its charitable status.",
    "Donor-data privacy is special-category-adjacent: donations reveal political, religious, social, or ideological beliefs (GDPR Art. 9 territory) and frequently come from vulnerable populations. Consent flows must default to opt-in, not opt-out; the right to be forgotten must work end-to-end including in any backed-up donation reports; sharing donor lists across organisations (e.g. partner-org cross-promotion) is itself a separate legal action that needs its own explicit consent.",
    "Restricted vs. unrestricted funds is a hard accounting boundary: when a donor gives \"for the new building\", that money cannot legally be spent on operating costs without violating the donor's intent — in the US a fiduciary breach, in Germany a gemeinnützigkeitsrechtliche Mittelfehlverwendung that can revoke charitable status. Any product touching donation tracking or budgeting must support fund-restriction tagging from day one, never let restricted balances aggregate into an unrestricted pool by default, and emit auditable reports showing both restricted and unrestricted use side-by-side.",
    "Mission-vs-platform trust is fragile: the donor / volunteer / member trusts the organisation because of its mission. The moment the platform is perceived as taking a cut, gating features behind paid tiers, surveilling participants, or making it easier to join than to leave, trust evaporates fast and openly (this audience writes about being mistreated, on principle). Be explicit about pricing or about being free, about who sees what data, and never use dark patterns on unsubscribe / delete / export.",
    "Operational reality is shoestring: most non-profits run on volunteer admins, no IT staff, the cheapest tooling that works, and aged hardware. A product that requires 2FA setup, current-browser features, mobile-first installation, or stable bandwidth will exclude the organisations that need it most. Default to \"works in a 2018 browser on a flaky connection\", document the offline / minimum-viable path explicitly, accept that the spec is set by the volunteer treasurer's laptop, not by the latest Chromium release."
  ],
  "government & civic": [
    "Public-records / FOIA exposure is a default, not an exception: most actions a public-sector body takes are potentially requestable under FOIA (US), IFG (Germany), Freedom of Information Act 2000 (UK), Access to Information legislation (Canada), and similar regimes. Any product holding government data must be audit-trail-complete (who-did-what-when, immutable), document its retention windows, and emit a one-click data-export usable in a public-records response. \"Right to be forgotten\" requests and FOIA disclosure can pull in opposite directions — design the conflict-resolution workflow up front, with a documented legal-counsel checkpoint.",
    "Accessibility is statute, not best-practice: WCAG 2.2 AA is the entry cost everywhere — ADA Title II in the US (binding from June 2026 for large public entities, June 2027 for smaller ones), Section 508 for federal procurement, the UK Public Sector Bodies (Websites and Mobile Applications) Accessibility Regulations 2018, EU EAA + EN 301 549, German BITV 2.0, plus state / Land-level rules layered on top. Procurement audits reject non-conforming products on the first slide — accessibility-conformance reports (VPAT in US, Konformitätserklärung in DE) are mandatory deliverables, not afterthoughts.",
    "Constituent-data privacy spans multiple, overlapping regimes: US Privacy Act of 1974 + state-by-state PII rules (CCPA / CPRA + Colorado / Connecticut / Virginia / Texas equivalents), German BDSG layered over GDPR with public-sector-specific Bundesdatenschutzgesetz §§ 22–35, UK Data Protection Act 2018, plus sector-specific overlays (CJIS for criminal-justice systems in the US, NHS Digital for UK health). Government data is also a state-actor target — threat-model assumptions that hold for private SaaS do not hold here.",
    "Procurement and vendor-compliance shape the whole product timeline: SOC 2 Type II is often a baseline, FedRAMP authorisation is mandatory for US federal cloud workloads (Moderate or High), German IT-Grundschutz / C5 / Schrems II-compatible hosting is mandatory for federal-sector workloads, ENS in Spain, CCN-CERT in defence-adjacent contexts. Sales cycles run 6–18 months. Lock-in clauses, opaque pricing, and seat-based upsells fail procurement review on first read — published per-resident or per-record pricing, no negotiated-only deals, and an explicit no-lock-in clause in the contract are entry-cost in this market.",
    "Election infrastructure is a separate compliance track: any feature touching voter registration, ballot delivery, vote tabulation, election-night reporting, or political-campaign data falls under election-specific oversight — EAC Voluntary Voting System Guidelines + state-by-state certification in the US, §§ 8–50 Bundeswahlgesetz (BWG) in Germany, AEC standards in Australia, Elections Canada in CA. Software touching election infrastructure carries criminal-liability exposure for tampering, mandatory air-gap or chain-of-custody requirements, and direct state oversight — never bundle it into an ordinary feature roadmap; it gets its own product, its own legal track, its own release cadence."
  ],
  "manufacturing": [
    "OT/IT boundary is safety-critical: Operational Technology (PLCs, SCADA, MES, robot controllers) was not built for internet connectivity. Connecting OT to IT without explicit segmentation (Purdue Enterprise Reference Architecture, zero-trust per zone) is the entry vector for ICS-class malware (Stuxnet, Industroyer, Triton). Any product sitting between IT and the shop floor must document where the boundary runs, who authenticates in which zone, what protocols cross the DMZ, and what happens when the WAN link goes down — IT-only assumptions break operations, OT-only assumptions break compliance.",
    "Safety compliance is non-negotiable: the EU Machinery Directive (2006/42/EC, replaced by Regulation (EU) 2023/1230 from January 2027), OSHA in the US, ISO 13849 for functional safety of control systems, IEC 61508 / 61511 for Safety Instrumented Systems. Software that touches machine control (PLC programming, e-stop logic, safety interlocks) qualifies as a safety component under the Machinery regime — that is a different compliance world from ordinary SaaS, with mandatory conformity assessment, EU Declarations of Conformity, often Notified-Body involvement. Treat \"the software might trigger a motor\" as a safety-case to be argued, not a feature.",
    "Industrial cybersecurity is now regulated, not best-practice: EU NIS2 Directive (transposition deadline October 2024, enforced country-by-country) makes operators of essential services accountable for cyber-risk management; IEC 62443 is the de-facto standard for industrial automation control systems; EU Cyber Resilience Act (CRA, applies from December 2027) obliges manufacturers of \"products with digital elements\" to vulnerability management, 24-hour incident notification, secure-by-default configuration, and SBOM disclosure. Industrial software inherits these obligations — design for SBOM emission, signed updates, and an actual incident-response playbook from the start.",
    "Equipment-data ownership and industrial data-spaces: who owns the data from a CNC machine — the OEM, the plant, the operator, the software vendor? The answer is inconsistent across the industry, often contractually contested, and increasingly governed by initiatives like GAIA-X, Manufacturing-X, and Catena-X (the German / EU sovereign-data-space programmes). These define specific requirements for data sovereignty, verifiable credentials, GAIA-X-conformant self-descriptions, and contractual data-usage controls. A product that exfiltrates equipment data without a clear, contractual ownership model is a procurement-killer in regulated EU manufacturing.",
    "Brownfield reality — old assets, long lifecycles, narrow bandwidth: industrial plants have 15–30-year lifecycles, much of the installed base runs Windows 7 / XP HMI panels, RS-232 / Modbus / OPC Classic interfaces, edge devices with 100 MB RAM and ARM Cortex-M. Many sites have intermittent or no WAN connectivity by IT-security policy. A product that assumes current browsers, cloud-first connectivity, gigabit links, or modern OPC UA-only fieldbuses is unusable for ~80 % of the installed base. Edge-first / offline-first / legacy-protocol-tolerant is the entry cost, not a polish feature."
  ],
  "HR & recruiting": [
    "Algorithmic-decision regulation has arrived: any product that scores, ranks, recommends, or auto-rejects candidates is now in regulated territory. **EU AI Act** classifies AI systems used for recruitment / promotion / termination as **high-risk** (Annex III, full obligations from August 2026 — risk management, data governance, technical documentation, human oversight, accuracy + robustness disclosure, post-market monitoring). **NYC Local Law 144** (in force since July 2023) requires an annual independent bias audit of any Automated Employment Decision Tool used on NYC residents and a candidate notice ≥ 10 business days before use. **Illinois AIVIA**, **Maryland HB 1202**, **Colorado AI Act** (effective February 2026) mirror this from different angles. \"Just a recommendation\" is not a defence — disparate impact under Title VII / EEOC + the four-fifths rule applies regardless of whether a human signed off the final decision.",
    "Background checks are a regulated workflow, not a feature toggle: in the US, the **Fair Credit Reporting Act** (FCRA) governs every consumer-report-based screen — pre-screen disclosure (standalone document), candidate authorisation, pre-adverse-action letter with the report and FTC summary of rights, a reasonable waiting period (5–7 business days is the practical floor), then the adverse-action letter. **EEOC enforcement guidance** + **ban-the-box** laws in 37+ US states / 150+ cities restrict when criminal-history questions can even be asked. In the EU, **GDPR Art. 10** restricts criminal-record processing to where authorised by law; the **UK DBS** regime is its own track with eligibility rules per role. A product that pulls a background check before the offer stage, or stores criminal history alongside ordinary HR data, breaks all of those — not as a UX polish issue but as litigation exposure.",
    "Employee data is special-category-adjacent and retention-conflicted: under **GDPR**, employment records are personal data with a lawful basis usually anchored in contract or legitimate interest, but health data (sick leave, accommodations), trade-union membership, and biometric access logs trip Art. 9 special-category protections. Retention is genuinely contested — payroll records have **statutory retention floors** (HMRC 6 years, IRS 4 years, German § 257 HGB 6–10 years, French Code du travail 5 years), but **GDPR right-to-erasure** and the **EU AI Act** training-data documentation requirements pull the other way. Add an **active litigation hold** and the picture gets worse. Build a per-data-class retention table at design time with the lawful basis and the deletion trigger encoded — not as a configuration option but as a schema constraint.",
    "Works-council / co-determination consultation rights are real and binding in much of Europe: in Germany, the **Betriebsrat** has co-determination rights under §§ 87, 90, 95 BetrVG over any technical system that monitors employee performance or behaviour — failure to consult invalidates the system's use, full stop. **France's CSE** under the Code du travail Article L.2312-8 has equivalent consultation rights on tools that track employees. **Austria, Netherlands, Sweden, Denmark, Finland, Belgium, Italy** each have parallel works-council consultation regimes. A recruiting / HR / performance product that ships in continental EU markets without a documented consultation flow for the customer's works council is unsold; \"we deal with that in implementation\" is not a viable market entry strategy.",
    "Candidate-data privacy is the trust-or-die surface: candidates entrust their CV, salary expectations, references, work-history gaps, citizenship status, and (in many cases) sensitive demographic data to a system they have no relationship with. **GDPR purpose limitation** is strict — data collected for one role cannot be silently re-used for another without re-consent or a separate lawful basis. **Talent-pool reuse** is an everyday GDPR trap: a candidate who applied 3 years ago is not in your talent pool by default unless they opted in to ongoing processing. **Right-to-erasure** must work end-to-end, including downstream copies in the ATS, the screening vendor, the email-marketing tool, and the analytics warehouse — not just the primary database. Candidates do file complaints; data protection authorities do enforce; fines under GDPR for HR violations have repeatedly hit seven figures (H&M €35.3M in 2020 for excessive employee monitoring is the canonical example)."
  ]
};

